{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery/settle-packet-v1.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery/settle-packet-v1.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"question\": {\n  \"question\": \"v1\",\n  \"about\": \"NVDA\",\n  \"kind\": \"asset\",\n  \"asks\": \"What total amount in USD of NVIDIA’s credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees or backstops to AI labs and cloud partners, including the support for the additional lab’s nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity, will be disclosed in NVIDIA’s FY2027 Form 10-K and related quantified disclosures by the annual report’s publication?\",\n  \"settles_on\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n  \"due\": \"2027-04-01\",\n  \"possible_answers\": [\n   {\n    \"answer\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"below 10,000,000,000 USD\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"from 10,000,000,000 USD to below 25,000,000,000 USD\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"from 25,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"100,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n \"what_the_judge_was_shown\": \"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount in USD of NVIDIA’s credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees or backstops to AI labs and cloud partners, including the support for the additional lab’s nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity, will be disclosed in NVIDIA’s FY2027 Form 10-K and related quantified disclosures by the annual report’s publication?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 10,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 10,000,000,000 USD to below 25,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 25,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 100,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 37 items]\\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e60, e274; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\\n- e38 [fact; Financial Times (reported via Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy 2026-09-22; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy, the SoftBank-backed developer, slowed preparations for a planned public listing that had been discussed around a $50 billion valuation, according to a September 22 Financial Times report, while a $4.9 billion debt package faced weak demand and yields around 10%.\\n    Quote: SB Energy's planned public listing is turning into a stress test for the financing side of the AI buildout. The SoftBank-backed developer has slowed preparations for an IPO that had been discussed around a $50 billion valuation, according to a September 22 Financial Times report, while a $4.9 billion debt package has faced weak demand and yields around 10%.\\n- e39 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA had already invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the developer's IPO.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) has already invested $1.5 billion and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the IPO.\\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e143; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e79 [third_party_view; Morningstar article author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f0206f98cb85dd776005198 2026-09-23] Morningstar, listing the year's major Nvidia news in September 2026, describes Nvidia's \\\"role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments\\\" as one of the threads around which its dividend announcement was overlooked. The article does not specify the form of that financing (equity, debt, credit support or guarantees), the counterparties, or the period covered.\\n    Quote: Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e118, e144, e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e92 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] AI cloud provider Nscale filed for a U.S. initial public offering on 18 September 2026 disclosing that it lost $1.02 billion in the first six months of 2026 on revenue of just $140.6 million — the financial condition of the counterparty to which NVIDIA is committing $1 billion of convertible financing.\\n    Quote: Nscale filed for a U.S. initial public offering on September 18 with one number that makes the AI-cloud boom look radioactive: the company lost $1.02 billion in the first six months of 2026 on just $140.6 million of revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e194; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e94 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] Nscale disclosed that as of 31 August 2026 it had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years, operating in 14 regions with more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity — the contracted demand base underlying the Nscale build-out NVIDIA supplies and is financing.\\n    Quote: As of August 31, Nscale had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts, with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years. It operates in 14 regions and has more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity.\\n- e95 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] Nscale disclosed that a single customer generated 52% of its first-half 2026 revenue, and its future plan leans heavily on Microsoft and Anthropic, indicating concentrated demand behind its contract book.\\n    Quote: Nscale disclosed that one customer generated 52% of first-half revenue, and its future plan leans heavily on Microsoft and Anthropic.\\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \\\"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e193 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing the Financial Times and Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale, the Nvidia-backed 'neocloud' headquartered in London, filed for a New York listing in the week before 24 September 2026 at a valuation of up to $35 billion, according to the Financial Times; it is asking investors to value it at as much as that on the promise that $103 billion in contracts, much of them not yet firm, will turn into revenue on schedule.\\n    Quote: This time it’s Nscale, the Nvidia-backed “neocloud” that filed for a New York listing last week at a valuation of up to $35 billion, according to the Financial Times . … Despite those losses, Nscale is asking Wall Street to value it at as much as $35 billion on the promise that $103 billion in contracts, much of them not yet firm, will turn into revenue on schedule.\\n- e194 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's S-1 reported a net loss of $1.02 billion for the six months to June 30, 2026, and at one point the company's own management flagged 'substantial doubt' about its ability to continue as a going concern.\\n    Quote: Per the S-1, the company posted a net loss of $1.02 billion in the six months to June 30, 2026—and at one point, its own management flagged “substantial doubt” about its ability to continue as a going concern.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e195 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale 2026-09-15; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] On Sept. 15, 2026, Nscale signed a subscription agreement for a $3.1 billion financing round, of which Nvidia itself is taking $1 billion in convertible notes or non-voting shares.\\n    Quote: On Sept. 15, Nscale signed a subscription agreement for a $3.1 billion financing round, of which Nvidia itself is taking $1 billion in convertible notes or non-voting shares.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n- e200 [fact; BioSpace, citing Basecamp Research's news release; original source Basecamp Research (issuer) and NVIDIA (participating investor) 2026-09-23; from gnews:c20b8b53b03e35fcff0718ce 2026-09-24] Basecamp Research closed a $140 million series C round, announced in a news release on Wednesday, in which NVIDIA participated as an investor; the oversubscribed round was led by venture capital firm S32. The item does not disclose the size of NVIDIA's individual investment.\\n    Quote: Basecamp Research has closed a $140 million series C fundraising to advance its pipeline of therapies designed using machine learning models. … The London- and Boston-based company is backed by NVIDIA, which participated in the oversubscribed round that was led by the venture capital firm S32, according to a news release on Wednesday .\\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\\n- e237 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, and management concedes some will call this circular financing while saying the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\n    Quote: Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e273 [fact; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, according to Trefis.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e274 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028; Trefis notes that part of the business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\n    Quote: Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\\n- e329 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic is reportedly seeking to raise as much as $100 billion in an IPO that might value it at roughly $2 trillion, and Reuters has since said the listing may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November; the article says the plans may change and neither company has confirmed the talks.\\n    Quote: The artificial intelligence (AI) company behind the Claude models is reportedly looking to raise as much as $100 billion in a listing that might value it at roughly $2 trillion. Reuters has since said that the IPO may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November. Of course, the plans might still change, and neither company has confirmed the talks.\\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e372 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, which the article describes as another large financing round for an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity and as evidence that GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure for the AI cloud operator.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, adding another large financing round to an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity. CoreWeave’s latest convertible financing showed how quickly GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure.\\n- e373 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nebius (as reported by cryptorank.io) ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] The article reports that Nebius has also turned to billions in financing and that the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt.\\n    Quote: The same issue extends across the sector. Nebius has also turned to billions in financing , while the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt .\\n- e374 [fact; cryptorank.io citing Reuters; original source Reuters ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Bond investors are noticing. Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require.\\n- e498 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as it seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, including potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies that would protect lenders if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to cover the debt, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the talks; the discussions are at an early stage and may not result in any deals.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held talks with insurance companies over ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as the chipmaker seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, the Financial Times reported on Tuesday. … The discussions include potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies, which could protect lenders if a borrower defaults and N\\n- e499 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, and is working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers, people familiar with the discussions told the Financial Times (Howden declined to comment); NVIDIA has also explored using insurance groups to syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, with the potential size of deals possibly exceeding the balance sheets of even large insurers, and has considered joining consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, while people familiar with the discussions said the company was also working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers. Howden declined to comment, the report said. … The potential structures could extend beyond traditional insurers. Nvidia has explored using insura\\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 3]\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) By January 31, 2027, will U.S. authorities authorize Nvidia to sell Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and will Chinese authorities permit those shipments?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s outlook: it excludes China data-center compute revenue, which is closest to rung 3, Still open; it is not a forecast of a formal prohibition. The evidence supports some route to Chinese purchases of the Blackwell-generation RTX PRO 5500, but the reported Chinese intent is u\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026, as shown by their Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures?: now rung 3 from 775,000,000,000 USD to below 825,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-28; reasons: The reference is nearly $800 billion, which sits in rung 3. The direct forecast still implies about $800 billion for the five in calendar 2026, within rung 3's $775 billion–below-$825 billion range. Alphabet's $200 billion is a component of the aggregate, not an additional amount; the GPU and capaci\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, or other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The available reference is no explicit consensus probability; the reported expectation that MIIT intends to approve some purchase plans, and that some firms were told approval is likely, places the reference at rung 1. I also forecast rung 1: those reports are specific to the firms and product in th\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the ladder’s no-reference baseline, rung 3 ($25bn to below $50bn). The strongest quantified evidence is a current maximum guarantee-obligation cap of $108.5bn, with the $105bn OpenAI lease guarantee treated as an example within that exposure, not added on top. That implies an amount above the $100bn rung-5 boundary. The newly quantified Nscale lease guarantee and the additional capacity and Aolani support add context, but there is no evidence that these sit outside the aggregate cap or a disclosed amount with which to add them. The other large financing and equity figures are not themselves credit-support commitments or minimum-revenue guarantees.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 98,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 203,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Drops Massive Number on Anthropic AI Spending\",\n   \"text\": \"Anthropics artificial-intelligence infrastructure commitments are becoming enormous, with Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) saying the AI developer now has more than $180 billion of contracted value across cloud providers and neocloud operators .\\nNvidia disclosed the figure during its latest Non-Deal Roadshow, offering a new look at just how aggressively Anthropic is locking in computing capacity as it competes with OpenAI and other frontier-model developers.\\nThe Dario Amodei-led company has also contracted for 2.5 gigawatts of Nvidia AI infrastructure scheduled for delivery through 2028.\\nThat is important for Nvidia because each gigawatt of AI infrastructure is becoming substantially more valuable to the chipmaker.\\nNvidia said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin .\\nNvidia continues to increase its share within the overall AI infrastructure market, the company said.\\nThe increase reflects more than higher-end GPUs. Nvidia is expanding into CPU racks for agentic AI, low-latency processing systems, networking and other infrastructure needed to operate what it describes as full-stack AI factories.\\nIts share of information-technology capital spending among the five largest cloud service providers has climbed from 11% in the first quarter of 2023 and is projected by Nvidia to reach 44% by 2027 .\\nNeoclouds are becoming another part of that expansion.\\nNvidia said Australian partners including IREN (IREN) and Firmus expect to bring roughly 2 gigawatts of capacity online by 2027 .\\nThe $180 billion figure is striking, but investors should distinguish Anthropics total contracted cloud and neocloud value from revenue going directly to Nvidia .\\nThe stronger Nvidia signal may instead be the combination of Anthropics 2.5GW commitment and Nvidias rapidly rising revenue opportunity per gigawatt.\\nIf Rubin-era systems really generate roughly $40 billion of opportunity per gigawatt, continued capacity commitments from frontier AI developers could dramatically expand Nvidias addressable market.\\nThe metrics to watch are therefore contracted gigawatts, Rubin deployments, cloud-provider capital spending and how much of each AI data center Nvidia captures beyond GPUs.\\nThat broader infrastructure opportunity helps explain why Nvidia simultaneously expanded its share-repurchase authorization by $150 billion to $235 billion while continuing to invest aggressively in AI.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"items\": [\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:T-116\",\n   \"stored_measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure (Blackwell generation)\",\n   \"stored_value\": \"$25 billion\",\n   \"stored_period\": null,\n   \"stored_kind\": \"estimate\",\n   \"stored_basis\": \"NVIDIA's estimate based on its reference designs; per gigawatt of AI infrastructure\",\n   \"stored_source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"article\": 203,\n   \"stored_passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin .\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:T-117\",\n   \"stored_measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure (Hopper generation)\",\n   \"stored_value\": \"roughly $18 billion\",\n   \"stored_period\": null,\n   \"stored_kind\": \"estimate\",\n   \"stored_basis\": \"NVIDIA's estimate based on its reference designs; per gigawatt of AI infrastructure\",\n   \"stored_source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"article\": 203,\n   \"stored_passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin .\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:T-118\",\n   \"stored_measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure (Rubin generation)\",\n   \"stored_value\": \"$40 billion\",\n   \"stored_period\": null,\n   \"stored_kind\": \"estimate\",\n   \"stored_basis\": \"NVIDIA's estimate based on its reference designs; per gigawatt of AI infrastructure\",\n   \"stored_source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"article\": 203,\n   \"stored_passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin .\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:T-119\",\n   \"stored_measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\",\n   \"stored_value\": \"$25 billion\",\n   \"stored_period\": null,\n   \"stored_kind\": \"estimate\",\n   \"stored_basis\": \"Management's estimate of NVIDIA revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, for Blackwell (comparison figure)\",\n   \"stored_source\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n   \"article\": 98,\n   \"stored_passages\": [\n    \"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:T-120\",\n   \"stored_measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\",\n   \"stored_value\": \"$40 billion per gigawatt\",\n   \"stored_period\": null,\n   \"stored_kind\": \"estimate\",\n   \"stored_basis\": \"Management's estimate of NVIDIA revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, for Vera Rubin\",\n   \"stored_source\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n   \"article\": 98,\n   \"stored_passages\": [\n    \"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
