{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery/settle-packet-v1.json\\\")); s=p[\\\"what_the_judge_was_shown\\\"]; print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(x for x in s.splitlines() if any(k in x.lower() for k in [\\\"gigawatt\\\", \\\"blackwell\\\", \\\"rubin\\\", \\\"hopper\\\", \\\"\"'$18 billion\", \"$25 billion\", \"$40 billion\"])))'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount in USD of NVIDIA’s credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees or backstops to AI labs and cloud partners, including the support for the additional lab’s nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity, will be disclosed in NVIDIA’s FY2027 Form 10-K and related quantified disclosures by the annual report’s publication?\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\n- e94 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] Nscale disclosed that as of 31 August 2026 it had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years, operating in 14 regions with more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity — the contracted demand base underlying the Nscale build-out NVIDIA supplies and is financing.\n    Quote: As of August 31, Nscale had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts, with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years. It operates in 14 regions and has more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity.\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) By January 31, 2027, will U.S. authorities authorize Nvidia to sell Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and will Chinese authorities permit those shipments?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s outlook: it excludes China data-center compute revenue, which is closest to rung 3, Still open; it is not a forecast of a formal prohibition. The evidence supports some route to Chinese purchases of the Blackwell-generation RTX PRO 5500, but the reported Chinese intent is u\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item\":\"v1:T-116\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The $25 billion per gigawatt estimate helps size the supported capacity already shown, making larger disclosed commitments more plausible. Revenue opportunity is not itself the guarantee amount.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v1:T-117\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The Hopper estimate offers an older-generation comparison for valuing the supported capacity, making larger commitments more plausible. The applicable generation and the guaranteed share are unknown.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v1:T-118\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The $40 billion per gigawatt estimate helps size the supported capacity already shown, making larger disclosed commitments more plausible. Revenue opportunity is not itself the guarantee amount.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v1:T-119\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The Blackwell estimate helps size the supported capacity already shown, making larger disclosed commitments more plausible. It does not establish the amount NVIDIA guarantees.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v1:T-120\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The Vera Rubin estimate helps size the supported capacity already shown, making larger disclosed commitments more plausible. It does not establish the amount NVIDIA guarantees.\"}"}}
